-- Q1: hard / L4 — Section 1248 gain recharacterization + Section 245A DRD interaction
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Calloway Corp, a domestic C corporation, sells 100% of its CFC stock for $8,000,000, realizing a $3,000,000 gain. The CFC has $2,200,000 of accumulated earnings and profits. Under Section 1248, $2,200,000 of the gain is recharacterized as a dividend. Calloway's tax advisor must evaluate the net tax impact considering the Section 245A DRD. Assess the combined effect of Sections 1248 and 245A on Calloway's taxable gain.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $2,200,000 recharacterized dividend qualifies for the Section 245A 100% DRD, leaving only $800,000 taxed as capital gain", "The entire $3,000,000 gain is taxed as ordinary income because Section 1248 overrides capital gain treatment entirely", "Section 245A does not apply to Section 1248 recharacterized dividends, so the full $3,000,000 is taxed as capital gain", "The $2,200,000 is taxed at qualified dividend rates and the remaining $800,000 is capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 1248, gain on the sale of CFC stock is recharacterized as dividend income to the extent of accumulated E&P ($2,200,000). Section 245A then allows a 100% DRD for this foreign-source dividend portion, effectively eliminating tax on it. The remaining $800,000 is taxed as capital gain. Wrong (B): Section 1248 recharacterizes gain only to the extent of E&P, not the full gain, and Section 245A offsets the dividend portion. Wrong (C): Section 245A explicitly applies to dividends arising under Section 1248 for C corporation shareholders. Wrong (D): C corporations do not receive qualified dividend rates; Section 245A provides a deduction, not a preferential rate.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — Section 954(c)(6) look-through rule for related CFC payments
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Harwick Holdings, a U.S. parent, owns CFC-1 and CFC-2. CFC-1 pays $500,000 in dividends to CFC-2 from earnings attributable entirely to active manufacturing operations. CFC-2's tax advisor must determine whether this dividend is Subpart F income (foreign personal holding company income). Apply the Section 954(c)(6) look-through rule to determine the Subpart F treatment of this payment.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The dividend is fully includable as Subpart F income because all inter-CFC dividends are automatically FPHCI", "The dividend is excluded from Subpart F income because the look-through rule treats it as attributable to CFC-1's active manufacturing income", "The dividend is excluded only if CFC-1 and CFC-2 are incorporated in the same country", "The dividend is partially excluded based on a 50/50 blended active and passive allocation"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 954(c)(6) provides a look-through rule under which dividends received from a related CFC are not treated as FPHCI to the extent attributable to the payor CFC's non-Subpart F income. Since CFC-1's income is entirely from active manufacturing, the full $500,000 dividend is excluded from Subpart F. Wrong (A): The look-through rule prevents automatic FPHCI classification when the payor's income is active. Wrong (C): The look-through rule applies regardless of country of incorporation and examines the character of the payor's underlying income. Wrong (D): There is no blended allocation; the rule traces the payment to the payor's actual income character.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Section 6038 penalty for failure to file Form 5471
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Dawson Corp, a U.S. shareholder of a CFC, fails to file Form 5471 for the current tax year. The IRS sends a notice demanding the filing, but Dawson does not respond within 90 days. Under Section 6038, what penalty does Dawson face for this failure?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A $1,000 penalty per year with no additional continuation penalties after notice", "A $5,000 penalty per annual accounting period with no escalation", "$10,000 per annual accounting period, plus $10,000 for each 30-day period of continued non-filing after notice (up to $50,000 additional)", "A $25,000 flat penalty per year with mandatory criminal referral to DOJ"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 6038(b) imposes a $10,000 penalty for each annual accounting period for failure to furnish information on Form 5471. If the failure continues more than 90 days after IRS notice, an additional $10,000 penalty applies for each 30-day period, up to a maximum additional $50,000. Wrong (A): The base penalty is $10,000, not $1,000, and continuation penalties apply. Wrong (B): The correct amount is $10,000 per period, not $5,000. Wrong (D): The standard penalty is $10,000 with scaled continuation penalties, not a flat $25,000 with criminal referral.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Section 884 branch profits tax definition
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 884, what is the branch profits tax?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A withholding tax on interest paid by foreign bank branches to U.S. depositors", "A penalty tax on U.S. corporations that maintain foreign branches", "A tax on undistributed earnings of domestic S corporations with foreign operations", "A 30% tax on foreign corporations operating a U.S. branch, imposed on the dividend equivalent amount to approximate the second-level withholding tax on repatriated earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 884 imposes a 30% branch profits tax on the dividend equivalent amount of a foreign corporation's effectively connected earnings and profits. This approximates the withholding tax that would apply if the branch were a subsidiary distributing dividends to its foreign parent. Wrong (A): The branch profits tax targets the foreign corporation's earnings removal, not interest payments to depositors. Wrong (B): Section 884 applies to foreign corporations operating U.S. branches, not U.S. corporations with foreign branches. Wrong (C): The branch profits tax has no connection to S corporation undistributed earnings.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L3 — Section 267(a)(3) matching rule for payments to related foreign persons
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Riverdale Inc., a domestic C corporation using the accrual method, accrues $600,000 in management fees payable to its foreign parent corporation in December of Year 1. The foreign parent uses the cash method and does not receive payment until March of Year 2. Apply Section 267(a)(3) to determine when Riverdale may deduct the management fees.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Riverdale deducts the $600,000 in Year 2 when the foreign parent includes the payment in income, because Section 267(a)(3) defers the deduction until the payee's inclusion", "Riverdale deducts the $600,000 in Year 1 when accrued because the accrual method controls timing", "Riverdale must capitalize the fees as a deferred charge and amortize them over 5 years", "Riverdale loses the deduction entirely because payments to related foreign persons are nondeductible"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 267(a)(3) provides a matching rule that defers a deduction for amounts owed to a related foreign person until the tax year in which the amount is includible in the payee's income. Since the foreign parent receives payment in Year 2, Riverdale's deduction is deferred to Year 2. Wrong (B): The accrual method does not control when Section 267(a)(3) applies; the statute overrides normal accrual timing for related foreign payees. Wrong (C): There is no capitalization or amortization requirement; the deduction is simply deferred. Wrong (D): The deduction is not permanently disallowed; it is deferred until the payee includes the amount in income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q6: easy / L2 — PFIC asset test under Section 1297(a)(2)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Greyfield Fund, a foreign corporation, has gross assets of $20,000,000. Of this, $11,000,000 consists of marketable securities and other passive assets, while $9,000,000 is used in active manufacturing. Greyfield's gross income is 60% from active operations and 40% from passive sources. Under the PFIC asset test of Section 1297(a)(2), does Greyfield qualify as a PFIC?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because a foreign corporation must have at least 75% passive assets to be a PFIC", "Yes, because at least 50% of Greyfield's average assets produce or are held for the production of passive income", "No, because the income test controls and only 40% of Greyfield's income is passive", "Yes, but only if the asset test and income test are both met simultaneously"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1297(a)(2) provides that a foreign corporation is a PFIC if 50% or more of its assets produce or are held to produce passive income. Greyfield's passive assets ($11,000,000) represent 55% of total assets, meeting the 50% threshold. Wrong (A): The 75% threshold applies to the income test under Section 1297(a)(1), not the asset test. Wrong (C): A corporation qualifies as a PFIC if either the income test or the asset test is met; they are alternatives, not cumulative. Wrong (D): Meeting either test alone is sufficient for PFIC classification.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q7: easy / L2 — QBAI uses alternative depreciation system (ADS)
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Prescott Corp's CFC owns manufacturing equipment with an original cost of $5,000,000. The equipment has been depreciated $2,000,000 under the modified accelerated cost recovery system (MACRS) and $1,500,000 under the alternative depreciation system (ADS). For purposes of computing QBAI under Section 951A(d), what adjusted basis should the CFC use for this equipment?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$3,000,000 (cost minus MACRS depreciation)", "Fair market value of the equipment at year-end", "$3,500,000 (cost minus ADS depreciation), because QBAI is determined using ADS", "The lesser of MACRS basis or ADS basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 951A(d)(2) requires that QBAI be determined using the alternative depreciation system (ADS) under Section 168(g), regardless of what depreciation method the CFC actually uses. ADS depreciation of $1,500,000 yields a $3,500,000 adjusted basis. Wrong (A): MACRS depreciation is not used for QBAI calculations; ADS is mandatory. Wrong (B): QBAI uses adjusted basis under ADS, not fair market value. Wrong (D): There is no lesser-of comparison; ADS is the sole method required by statute.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Section 863(b) sourcing of production income
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Ashton Manufacturing, a U.S. corporation, produces goods entirely in the United States and sells them to unrelated customers in Country G. Ashton has no fixed place of business in Country G. Under the sourcing rules of Section 863(b) and the regulations, Ashton must determine how the production income is sourced for purposes of the FTC limitation. Apply the sourcing rules to classify this income.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The income is entirely foreign-source because the sales occur in Country G", "The income is entirely U.S.-source because title passes in the United States", "The income is split between U.S. and foreign sources based on where production activities and title passage occur", "The income is entirely U.S.-source because all production activities occur in the United States"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Treas. Reg. Section 1.863-3, when production activities occur entirely in one country, all income from the sale is sourced to that country regardless of where title passes. Since Ashton produces entirely in the United States, the income is 100% U.S.-source. Wrong (A): Sales location alone does not determine sourcing when production is entirely in one country. Wrong (B): While the result is U.S.-source, the controlling factor is production location, not title passage. Wrong (C): A production/title split applies only when production activities occur in multiple countries.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Subpart F income inclusion timing
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 951(a), when must a U.S. shareholder include its pro rata share of a CFC's Subpart F income in gross income?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["On the last day of the CFC's taxable year on which the foreign corporation qualifies as a CFC", "On the date the CFC distributes a dividend to the U.S. shareholder", "On the first day of the U.S. shareholder's taxable year following the CFC's earning period", "On the date the CFC files its foreign tax return"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 951(a)(1) requires a U.S. shareholder to include its pro rata share of Subpart F income for the taxable year of the CFC that ends with or within the U.S. shareholder's taxable year, determined as of the last day the corporation is a CFC. Wrong (B): Subpart F income is included currently regardless of whether any distribution is made. Wrong (C): The inclusion occurs in the year the CFC's taxable year ends, not the following year. Wrong (D): The CFC's foreign tax return filing date has no bearing on the U.S. shareholder's inclusion timing.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: medium / L2 — Section 245A(e) hybrid dividends
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Langford Corp, a domestic C corporation, receives a $1,000,000 dividend from its wholly-owned CFC. The CFC's payment is treated as a deductible expense in the CFC's country of incorporation, reducing the CFC's foreign tax liability. Under Section 245A(e), how does this "hybrid" character affect Langford's eligibility for the Section 245A DRD?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The full $1,000,000 qualifies for the 100% DRD regardless of foreign tax treatment", "The $1,000,000 dividend is ineligible for the Section 245A DRD because it is a hybrid dividend that was deductible by the CFC in its home country", "Only 50% of the dividend is eligible for the DRD when the payment is deductible abroad", "The dividend is reclassified as interest income and subject to withholding tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 245A(e) denies the 100% DRD for hybrid dividends — amounts received from a CFC that are also deductible by the CFC in its country of incorporation. This prevents a double tax benefit where income escapes both U.S. and foreign taxation. Wrong (A): The DRD is specifically denied for hybrid dividends under Section 245A(e). Wrong (C): There is no 50% partial DRD for hybrid dividends; the entire amount is ineligible. Wrong (D): Hybrid dividends remain classified as dividends, not interest; the provision denies the DRD rather than reclassifying the income.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Subpart F insurance income
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Radcliffe Holdings, a U.S. parent, owns a CFC incorporated in Country J that insures risks located in Country K (outside Country J). The premiums earned from these policies total $3,000,000. Under the Subpart F income rules, how is this $3,000,000 classified?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Active business income excluded from Subpart F because insurance is an operating business", "Foreign base company services income because insurance involves providing services to policyholders", "Subpart F insurance income under Section 953, because the CFC insures risks located outside its country of incorporation", "Foreign personal holding company income because insurance premiums are passive in nature"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 953 defines Subpart F insurance income as income attributable to the issuing or reinsuring of insurance or annuity contracts in connection with risks located outside the CFC's country of incorporation. The CFC in Country J insuring Country K risks triggers this category. Wrong (A): Insurance income from risks outside the CFC's home country is specifically targeted by Subpart F, not excluded as active business income. Wrong (B): Insurance income has its own Subpart F category under Section 953 and is not classified as services income. Wrong (D): Insurance premiums are classified under the insurance income category, not FPHCI.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Section 909 foreign tax credit splitter arrangements
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Elwood Corp, a U.S. parent, owns CFC-Alpha. CFC-Alpha pays $400,000 in foreign taxes to Country L, but a hybrid arrangement causes the related income to be attributed to CFC-Beta (owned by an unrelated foreign person) under Country L's laws. Elwood claims the $400,000 as deemed-paid foreign tax credits. Under Section 909, evaluate whether Elwood may claim the credit in the current year.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Elwood may claim the full $400,000 credit because CFC-Alpha paid the taxes", "Elwood may claim only 50% of the credit under the splitter limitation", "Elwood may claim the credit only if CFC-Beta is also a CFC of a U.S. shareholder", "Elwood may not claim the credit until the related income is taken into account by the same person that paid the tax, because Section 909 suspends credits for splitter arrangements"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 909 addresses foreign tax credit splitter arrangements where foreign taxes are paid by one person but the related income is taken into account by a different person. Credits are suspended until the related income is recognized by the taxpayer claiming the credit, preventing a mismatch. Wrong (A): Simply paying the tax is insufficient; Section 909 requires matching the tax to the related income in the same hands. Wrong (B): Section 909 suspends the full credit, not a partial 50% limitation. Wrong (C): CFC-Beta's ownership is irrelevant; the suspension applies until the income and tax are matched in the same taxpayer.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — QBAI definition
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$For purposes of the GILTI computation under Section 951A, what does qualified business asset investment (QBAI) represent?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The average of a CFC's aggregate adjusted bases in specified tangible depreciable property, determined using the alternative depreciation system", "The fair market value of all CFC assets including intangibles and goodwill", "The total amount of a CFC's capital stock and retained earnings", "The aggregate adjusted bases of all CFC property including inventory and accounts receivable"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 951A(d) defines QBAI as the average of a CFC's aggregate adjusted bases, determined under ADS, in specified tangible property used in a trade or business and subject to depreciation. It excludes intangibles, goodwill, and non-depreciable assets. Wrong (B): QBAI uses adjusted basis under ADS, not fair market value, and excludes intangibles and goodwill. Wrong (C): QBAI measures tangible depreciable asset bases, not capital stock or retained earnings. Wrong (D): QBAI is limited to tangible depreciable property and excludes inventory, receivables, and other non-depreciable assets.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Section 338(g) election for foreign target acquisition
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Benton Corp, a U.S. corporation, acquires 100% of a foreign corporation's stock for $15,000,000 in a qualified stock purchase. The foreign corporation has depreciable assets with a low adjusted basis and high fair market value. Benton's tax advisor recommends a Section 338(g) election. What is the primary effect of this election?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["It converts the stock purchase into a tax-free reorganization under Section 368", "It creates a deemed asset sale at fair market value, stepping up the foreign target's asset bases without triggering U.S. tax on the foreign seller", "It allows Benton to amortize the acquisition goodwill over 15 years without an asset purchase", "It defers gain recognition on the acquisition until the target's assets are subsequently sold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): A Section 338(g) election treats the foreign target as having sold all of its assets at fair market value on the acquisition date and repurchased them the next day. For a foreign target, the deemed sale typically does not generate U.S. tax, but the buyer obtains a stepped-up basis in the target's assets for future depreciation and gain calculations. Wrong (A): Section 338 does not create a tax-free reorganization; it creates a deemed asset sale. Wrong (C): Goodwill amortization from a stock purchase requires a Section 338 election to step up basis, but the election's primary effect is the deemed sale-repurchase. Wrong (D): The election triggers immediate deemed recognition, not deferral.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Section 367(b) inbound transaction rules
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Brighton Corp, a U.S. corporation, owns 100% of a CFC with $3,000,000 of accumulated earnings and profits. The CFC liquidates into Brighton under Section 332. Under Section 367(b) and the regulations, the transaction is an inbound transfer. Apply Section 367(b) to determine the U.S. tax consequence for Brighton.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Brighton recognizes no income because Section 332 liquidations are always tax-free to the parent", "Brighton recognizes capital gain equal to the difference between FMV and basis in the CFC stock", "Brighton must include the CFC's $3,000,000 of untaxed accumulated E&P as a deemed dividend under the Section 367(b) regulations", "Brighton may elect to defer recognition of the CFC's E&P until a subsequent disposition of the assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 367(b) regulations (Treas. Reg. Section 1.367(b)-3), when a CFC liquidates into its U.S. corporate parent, the U.S. shareholder must include the all earnings and profits amount attributable to the CFC stock as a deemed dividend, to the extent the CFC has untaxed E&P. Wrong (A): Section 332 nonrecognition is modified by Section 367(b) for inbound transactions involving CFCs with untaxed E&P. Wrong (B): The inclusion is characterized as a deemed dividend, not capital gain. Wrong (D): There is no deferral election; the E&P inclusion is mandatory in the year of the inbound transaction.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Treaty tie-breaker rules for dual-resident individuals
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Morrison, a U.S. citizen, is also considered a tax resident of Country M under Country M's domestic law. Morrison maintains a permanent home only in Country M, has closer personal and economic ties to Country M, and habitually resides in Country M. Under the U.S.-Country M income tax treaty tie-breaker provisions, how is Morrison's residency resolved for treaty purposes?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Morrison is treated as a resident solely of the United States because U.S. citizenship always takes priority", "Morrison's residency is split equally between both countries for treaty purposes", "The treaty tie-breaker does not apply to U.S. citizens, so Morrison remains a dual resident subject to the saving clause", "Morrison is treated as a resident of Country M under the treaty tie-breaker because permanent home and center of vital interests are in Country M"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): While treaty tie-breaker provisions would assign residency to Country M based on permanent home and center of vital interests, most U.S. treaties contain a saving clause that preserves the U.S. right to tax its citizens. In practice, Morrison is treated as a Country M resident for treaty benefit purposes, but the U.S. saving clause still allows full U.S. taxation. The tie-breaker determination itself favors Country M. Wrong (A): Citizenship alone does not override the treaty tie-breaker analysis, though the saving clause preserves U.S. taxing rights. Wrong (B): Treaties resolve dual residency to one country, not a split. Wrong (C): The tie-breaker does apply; the saving clause is a separate mechanism that preserves U.S. taxing jurisdiction.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Section 898 required taxable year for specified foreign corporations
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Under Section 898, what taxable year is required for a specified foreign corporation (a CFC with a majority U.S. shareholder)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["A taxable year that conforms to the required year of the majority U.S. shareholder, unless the CFC establishes a business purpose for a different year", "Any 12-month period chosen by the CFC's board of directors", "A calendar year regardless of the U.S. shareholder's tax year", "The taxable year of the foreign country where the CFC is incorporated"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 898 requires a specified foreign corporation to use a taxable year that conforms to the required year of its majority U.S. shareholder, minimizing deferral. A different year may be used only if a business purpose is established to the IRS's satisfaction. Wrong (B): The CFC cannot freely choose any year; Section 898 mandates conformity with the majority U.S. shareholder's year. Wrong (C): A calendar year is not automatically required; the CFC must match its majority U.S. shareholder's taxable year. Wrong (D): The foreign country's conventions do not control the CFC's required U.S. tax year.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: hard / L4 — BEAT: R&D credit interaction with adjusted regular tax liability
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Stanton Corp, a U.S. corporation with $800 million in average gross receipts, has regular tax liability of $12,000,000 and $3,000,000 in R&D tax credits (Section 41). Its BEAT amount (10% of modified taxable income) is $14,000,000. Under Section 59A, the BEAT applies when the BEAT amount exceeds the "adjusted regular tax liability," which is regular tax reduced by most credits but allows certain credits (including R&D) to reduce regular tax without reducing the BEAT comparison base. Evaluate Stanton's additional BEAT liability.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stanton owes $5,000,000 additional BEAT because BEAT amount ($14,000,000) exceeds regular tax minus all credits ($9,000,000)", "Stanton owes $2,000,000 additional BEAT because the R&D credit is excluded from the adjusted regular tax liability reduction, so adjusted regular tax is $12,000,000 and BEAT excess is $2,000,000", "Stanton owes no additional BEAT because R&D credits fully offset the BEAT liability", "Stanton owes $14,000,000 because BEAT replaces regular tax entirely when it exceeds the threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 59A(b)(1)(B), the BEAT applies when the BEAT amount exceeds the adjusted regular tax liability. For applicable taxpayers, certain credits including R&D credits under Section 41 are allowed to reduce regular tax without reducing the BEAT comparison base. Therefore, the adjusted regular tax liability remains $12,000,000 (not reduced by R&D credits for BEAT comparison purposes). The BEAT excess is $14,000,000 minus $12,000,000 = $2,000,000 additional tax. Wrong (A): The R&D credit does not reduce the adjusted regular tax liability for BEAT purposes, so the comparison base is $12,000,000, not $9,000,000. Wrong (C): R&D credits cannot offset the BEAT; the BEAT is a separate minimum tax that applies when it exceeds the adjusted regular tax. Wrong (D): The BEAT is an add-on tax for the excess amount, not a replacement of the entire regular tax liability.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — GILTI tested loss mechanics
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Quinton Corp owns three CFCs. CFC-1 has $4,000,000 of tested income, CFC-2 has a tested loss of $1,200,000, and CFC-3 has $2,000,000 of tested income. Aggregate QBAI from the tested income CFCs is $15,000,000. Under the GILTI rules, how does CFC-2's tested loss affect Quinton's GILTI inclusion?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The tested loss is ignored because only tested income CFCs are included in GILTI", "The tested loss carries forward to offset CFC-2's future tested income", "CFC-2's tested loss of $1,200,000 reduces aggregate net CFC tested income to $4,800,000, but CFC-2's QBAI is excluded from the aggregate QBAI calculation", "The tested loss reduces both aggregate tested income and aggregate QBAI proportionally"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 951A(c)(1), net CFC tested income equals aggregate tested income minus aggregate tested losses. CFC-2's $1,200,000 loss reduces aggregate tested income from $6,000,000 to $4,800,000. However, under Section 951A(d), only CFCs with tested income contribute QBAI; CFC-2's QBAI is excluded. Wrong (A): Tested losses are not ignored; they reduce aggregate tested income in the GILTI computation. Wrong (B): Tested losses do not carry forward; they offset other CFCs' tested income in the current year. Wrong (D): QBAI is not reduced proportionally; tested loss CFCs simply do not contribute QBAI to the aggregate.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: hard / L3 — Section 1503(d) domestic use agreement for dual consolidated losses
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Whitfield Corp, a U.S. parent, operates a branch in Country N that generates a $3,000,000 loss. Country N's tax rules allow a related Country N entity to use this same loss against its local income. Under the dual consolidated loss rules of Section 1503(d), Whitfield wants to use the $3,000,000 loss against its U.S. consolidated group income. The tax advisor considers filing a domestic use agreement. Apply Section 1503(d) to determine whether Whitfield can claim the loss in the United States.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Whitfield may freely use the loss in both jurisdictions because international double deductions are permitted", "Whitfield must reduce the loss by 50% before claiming it on the U.S. return", "Whitfield may never use a dual consolidated loss against U.S. income under any circumstances", "Whitfield may use the loss against U.S. income by filing a domestic use agreement certifying the loss will not be used to offset income of any foreign person, with recapture if the agreement is violated"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Section 1503(d) generally prohibits using a dual consolidated loss to offset income of a domestic affiliate. However, a domestic use agreement allows the U.S. use if the taxpayer certifies the loss will not also be used to offset income of a foreign person. If the loss is subsequently used abroad, recapture provisions require the loss to be added back to U.S. income. Wrong (A): Section 1503(d) specifically prohibits double use of the same loss in two jurisdictions. Wrong (B): There is no 50% reduction mechanism; the loss is either fully allowed (with a domestic use agreement) or fully denied. Wrong (C): The domestic use agreement provides an exception to the general prohibition, allowing U.S. use under specific conditions.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Section 304 cross-border related corporation redemption
(6, 'International Tax', $EXPL$Drummond, a U.S. individual, owns 100% of both USCo (a domestic C corporation) and ForeignCo (a CFC with $2,000,000 of accumulated E&P). Drummond sells his ForeignCo stock to USCo for $3,000,000 cash. Because Drummond controls both corporations, Section 304(a)(1) recharacterizes this transaction. Under Section 304, the transfer is treated as a contribution of ForeignCo stock to USCo followed by a redemption. The redemption fails the Section 302(b) tests because Drummond maintains 100% control. Evaluate the tax consequences to Drummond.$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The $3,000,000 is treated as a dividend to the extent of the combined E&P of both corporations, with ForeignCo's E&P ($2,000,000) considered first, followed by USCo's E&P", "The $3,000,000 is treated as capital gain because stock sales between related parties are always capital transactions", "Section 304 does not apply to cross-border transactions involving CFCs", "The $3,000,000 is treated as a return of basis with no dividend consequences"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 304(a)(1), when a person controls both the acquiring and issuing corporations, the transfer is recharacterized as a Section 351 contribution followed by a redemption. If the redemption fails Section 302(b), the payment is treated as a distribution (dividend to the extent of E&P). Section 304(b)(2) requires testing the acquired corporation's E&P first (ForeignCo's $2,000,000), then the acquiring corporation's E&P. Wrong (B): Section 304 specifically overrides capital gain treatment when the redemption fails Section 302(b) tests. Wrong (C): Section 304 applies to cross-border transactions and is commonly used by the IRS to recharacterize related-party stock transfers involving CFCs. Wrong (D): There is no return-of-basis treatment when the redemption is treated as a dividend; basis is added to the acquiring corporation's stock.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4)

;

COMMIT;